sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/hamilton-rule> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>, <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/TestableHypothesis>;
    dc:description "A gene for social behaviour increases in frequency when the benefit to the recipient, weighted by genetic relatedness, exceeds the cost to the actor.";
    dc:source "W. D. Hamilton (1964) The genetical evolution of social behaviour. I";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Hamilton's rule";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature09205>, <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1420-9101.2011.02236.x>;
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "\\Delta \\bar{p} > 0 \\iff r b - c > 0, with r, b, c defined as regression coefficients (Queller form via the Price equation)";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Assumptions: in Hamilton's original derivation, additive fitness effects and weak selection; in the general form, b and c are partial regressions of fitness on the actor's and the partner's genotype. Formal status: proved; exact in the regression form (Gardner, West and Wild 2011). Critics argue that the general form is close to an identity with little predictive content (Nowak, Tarnita and Wilson 2010).";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hypothesisType> "model-theorem";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/proposedTest> "In a cooperative system (social insects, cooperatively breeding birds, microbes), estimate r from genotypes and b and c from fitness measurements, and test whether rb > c predicts which individuals help."
}
